@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_GoTo}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Overview}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_GoTo @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Overview</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_GoTo @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Overview . . . In Progress . . .</h1>

<p>
    The GoTo component gives keyboard users a way to jump straight to the parts of a page that matter to them. You place a <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;GoTo /&gt;</span> marker,
    with a short label such as "Filters" or "Order summary", at each destination. A single <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;GoToViewer /&gt;</span>, added once to your layout, lists the markers
    on the current page. It opens with <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Ctrl + Shift + Y</kbd>, or with a trigger button that stays hidden until it receives keyboard focus,
    so it adds nothing to the page for anyone who doesn't need it.
</p>

<p>
    Screen reader users can move around a page by headings and landmarks, but keyboard users who can see the page have little more than the
    <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Tab</kbd> key, which visits every link and control on the way. Skip links help, but they usually only skip forward to the main content.
    The GoTo viewer works from anywhere on the page, in either direction, and because you choose the destinations, they can be named after what the user is looking for and not just after how the page
    happens to be built.
</p>

<p>
    While the viewer is open, every marker on the page shows a numbered badge, and the list shows the same number beside each label. Moving to a button in the list, with
    <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Tab</kbd> or the arrow keys, scrolls the matching marker into view and outlines its badge, so the user can see where they will land
    before they commit. Activating a button closes the viewer, moves to the marker and displays its label, with focus on the label so that screen readers announce where the user has arrived.
    The label is shown in the top layer, so it isn't clipped by the container the marker sits in, and it closes as soon as focus moves on. <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Tab</kbd>
    then continues from that point on the page.
</p>

<p>
    The viewer is a non-modal popover. It closes with <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Escape</kbd>, when focus leaves it, or when a marker is chosen, and it never traps focus.
    Tabbing out of it continues to the next element on the page. The list is built each time the viewer opens, so it always reflects what is on the page at that moment, including content that appears
    after a route change or a user action. Markers that can't currently be reached, such as those inside a collapsed panel or content hidden with <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">display: none</span>,
    are left out, so the list never contains a button that does nothing.
</p>

<p>
    A marker takes up no space on the page. It's an empty, zero-size element, so it won't change your layout, and it's best placed between blocks of content, for example directly before the heading of a section.
    Avoid placing markers directly inside lists, tables, or flex and grid containers that use a gap, where even a zero-size element is treated as an item. Markers placed inside a modal dialog are not supported,
    and the keyboard shortcut does nothing while a modal dialog is open.
</p>

<p>
    The heading, the button text and the message shown when a page has no markers can all be changed, which also makes them easy to localise. The trigger button can be left out if you would rather rely
    on the keyboard shortcut alone. Markers scroll into view with a small offset from the top of the screen, so a fixed header is less likely to cover them, and the offset can be changed with a CSS variable.
</p>

<p>
    The component requires an interactive render mode, as the list of markers is built in the browser and passed back to Blazor. If you need a link at the top of the page that bypasses repeated content,
    see the <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Class" href="@($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_SkipTo}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Overview}")">@GlobalValues.Component_Name_SkipTo</a>
    component, which GoTo complements rather than replaces.
</p>

<div class="@FlexContent.Base @FlexContent.Wrap(UnitFlexWrap.Wrap) @LayoutAlignment.JustifyContent(UnitJustifyContent.SpaceBetween) @LayoutAlignment.AlignItems(UnitAlignItems.Centre)">
    <a class="@Button.Base @Button.LinkStyle @Gap.SetColGap(UnitGapSize.Four)" href="@($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_Disclosure}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Usage}")">
        <span class="@SvgIcon.Base @SvgIcon.Size(UnitIconSize.ExtraLarge)" aria-hidden="true" style="--_svg-icon-source:var(--svg-left-arrow-circle-icon)"></span>
        <span>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Disclosure - @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Usage</span>

    </a>
    <a class="@Button.Base @Button.LinkStyle @Gap.SetColGap(UnitGapSize.Four)" href="@($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_GoTo}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Installation}")">

        <span>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_GoTo - @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Installation</span>
        <span class="@SvgIcon.Base @SvgIcon.Size(UnitIconSize.ExtraLarge)" aria-hidden="true" style="--_svg-icon-source:var(--svg-right-arrow-circle-icon)"></span>
    </a>
</div>


